---
import Banner from "../components/astro/content/Banner.astro";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "../components/astro/content/Header.astro";
import AboutMe from "../components/astro/content/AboutMe.astro";
import Projects from "../components/astro/Projects.astro";
import Footer from "../components/astro/content/Footer.astro";
import CookieBanner from "../components/react/CookieBanner.jsx";
import { getLangFromUrl, useTranslations } from "../i18n/utils";
import MyStory from "../components/astro/content/MyStory.astro";
import WhyWeb from "../components/astro/content/WhyWeb.astro";
import Link from "../components/astro/Link.astro";
import GitHub from "../components/icons/GitHub";
import LinkedIn from "../components/icons/LinkedIn";

const t = useTranslations(getLangFromUrl(Astro.url));
---

<Layout>
  <Header />
  <Banner />
  <section class="px-4 max-w-4xl mx-auto my-12 md:my-24">
    <h2 id="about-me" class="text-5xl mb-6 font-title font-black tracking-wide">
      {t("About me")}
    </h2>
    <MyStory />

    <h3 class="text-3xl mb-6 font-title font-black tracking-wide">
      {t("Why web programming?")}
    </h3>
    <WhyWeb />
    <!-- SECTION: WHY ALTERFACE -->
    <section id="why-alterface">
      <h3 class="text-3xl mb-6 font-title font-black tracking-wide">
        Why Alterface?
      </h3>

      <p
        class="text-lg text-justify text-black leading-8 tracking-wide mb-8 italic"
      >
        "Because where else can a biochemist-turned-developer find a company
        that literally builds magic for a living?"
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-justify text-black leading-8 tracking-wide mb-8">
        Let me be honest: I didn't grow up dreaming of becoming a software
        engineer. I grew up dreaming of becoming a scientist. And I did — until
        life had other plans and I discovered that code is just another kind of
        experiment. So when I found Alterface, something clicked. You're not
        building another CRUD app or yet another SaaS dashboard. You're building
        experiences that make people gasp, laugh, and forget reality for a few
        minutes. That's the kind of work where the "why" matters as much as the
        "how."
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-justify text-black leading-8 tracking-wide mb-8">
        Legacy code doesn't scare me — it intrigues me. My entire thesis was 3
        years of digging into someone else's research, finding patterns, and
        improving on them. Your Salto language and existing codebases? That's
        not a barrier; that's an adventure. C# is my home turf: I built
        CatCareApi with ASP.NET Core, Entity Framework, and SQL Server, and I
        know what good architecture looks like even if I'm still leveling up.
        I'm ready to learn your stack and your ways.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-justify text-black leading-8 tracking-wide mb-8">
        Documentation is in my DNA. Coming from academia, I was trained to write
        thorough, precise technical documentation because if it wasn't
        documented, it didn't happen. You need installation docs for clients and
        internal teams? I actually enjoy that. And on-site installations around
        the world? I've relocated across four countries, adapted to new cultures
        and languages more times than I can count. Traveling to install
        attractions in places I've never been sounds like the best job perk ever
        invented.
      </p>

      <p
        class="text-lg text-justify text-black leading-8 tracking-wide mb-8 italic"
      >
        And let's be practical: I live in Belgium. Wavre is practically down the
        road from Perwez. This isn't a "maybe someday I'll move" situation — I'm
        already here, ready to go. Alterface sits at the intersection of
        creativity, engineering, and real-world wonder. That's exactly where I
        want to build my career — not behind a screen pushing pixels, but
        building things that make people feel something.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION: WHY ME? -->
    <section id="why-me" class="mt-16">
      <h3 class="text-3xl mb-6 font-title font-black tracking-wide">Why Me?</h3>

      <p
        class="text-lg text-justify text-black leading-8 tracking-wide mb-8 italic"
      >
        "Because I debug proteins AND code — and honestly, proteins are harder."
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-justify text-black leading-8 tracking-wide mb-8">
        Here's the deal: I'm a junior developer. I won't pretend I have 10 years
        of experience shipping enterprise software. What I do have is a profile
        that most candidates don't. I learned how to learn — the hard way.
        Biochemistry to web development isn't a "I watched a tutorial on
        YouTube" transition. It was months of disciplined study, structured
        training, and building real projects from scratch. The same
        hypothesis-driven mindset that helped me investigate genetic mutations
        in a German research lab now helps me track down bugs in a Node.js
        backend or debug a C# controller.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-justify text-black leading-8 tracking-wide mb-8">
        I've worked in legacy before — it just wasn't called code. Three years
        of undergraduate research meant inheriting protocols, deciphering other
        people's methodologies, and figuring out how to extend work that came
        before me. Sound familiar? That's exactly what you're describing in your
        job post: "dive into legacy code, adding new features and fixing bugs."
        I've been doing a version of that my whole career.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-justify text-black leading-8 tracking-wide mb-8">
        I take ownership. End to end. My thesis wasn't a group assignment — I
        managed data collection, analysis, technical reporting, and publication
        prep for three years. At Alterface, you want engineers who can take a
        project from specification to on-site installation. That kind of
        full-cycle ownership isn't new to me; it's how I've always worked. And
        I'm multilingual and multicultural — for real. Spanish native, English
        C1, French B2 (and climbing). I've lived in Chile, Germany, France, and
        Belgium. Your clients and partners are global, your on-site
        installations take you all over the world, and communication matters to
        you. I'm not just comfortable in international environments — I thrive
        in them.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-justify text-black leading-8 tracking-wide mb-8">
        And yes — I'm still hungry. I'm currently picking up Python (because you
        mentioned it), sharpening TypeScript, and building a full-stack client
        project in Laravel + React. I'm not someone who finished a bootcamp and
        stopped. I'm someone who treats learning like breathing. What I lack in
        years of professional dev experience, I make up for in scientific rigor
        and analytical thinking, international adaptability, natural-born
        documentation skills, genuine love for debugging and problem-solving,
        and a deep, slightly stubborn drive to get things right.
      </p>

      <p
        class="text-lg text-justify text-black leading-8 tracking-wide mb-8 italic"
      >
        You're not just hiring a junior developer. You're hiring someone who
        already reinvented herself once — and is ready to do it again, this time
        building attractions that make people smile. That sounds like a pretty
        good deal to me.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="flex flex-col md:flex-row gap-4 mt-8 md:items-center">
      <span class="text-xl font-black">{t("Find me on")}:</span>
      <div class="flex gap-4">
        <Link
          title={t("My GitHub repository")}
          className=" bg-white px-3 py-2"
          target="_blank"
          href="https://github.com/cararcel"
          ><GitHub className="size-6" />GitHub
        </Link>
        <Link
          title={t("My LinkedIn profile")}
          className="  bg-blue-600 text-white px-3 py-2"
          target="_blank"
          href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/arce-camila/"
          ><LinkedIn className="size-6 fill-current" /> LinkedIn
        </Link>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <Projects />
  <Footer />
  <CookieBanner client:load trans={t("cookie")} />
</Layout>
